(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-1-micro-exam-1-exam-style-quiz"]={"title":"Microbiology Exam 1 — Exam-Style Review Quiz","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A Gram-positive organism is isolated from a wound. Which agent is it especially vulnerable to?","choices":["Nothing that acts on the wall","Polymyxin","An aminoglycoside alone","Lysozyme"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Its thick peptidoglycan is the enzyme's substrate and lies exposed as the outermost layer, with no outer membrane covering it. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 24-29"},{"q":"Which wall feature makes Gram-negative organisms resistant to many agents that kill Gram-positive ones?","choices":["An outer membrane","A thicker peptidoglycan layer","Teichoic acid","A capsule"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It acts as a permeability barrier, excluding large and hydrophobic molecules before they can reach the peptidoglycan beneath. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 26-29"},{"q":"What is released from a Gram-negative organism when the cell lyses?","choices":["Exotoxin","Lipid A","Lysozyme","Teichoic acid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lipid A is the toxic portion of the lipopolysaccharide built into the outer membrane, so it only reaches the host when the cell breaks apart. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 26-29"},{"q":"What makes mycoplasmas unusual among bacteria?","choices":["They have two cell walls","They have no ribosomes","They have no cell wall","They have no genetic material"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The absence of peptidoglycan is the single feature from which everything else about them follows. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 14-18"},{"q":"Because mycoplasmas lack a cell wall, which drug class is useless against them?","choices":["The macrolides","The penicillins","The tetracyclines","The fluoroquinolones"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A penicillin blocks crosslinking of peptidoglycan, so an organism that builds no peptidoglycan offers the drug no target at all. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 14-18"},{"q":"Which structure allows a bacterium to survive heat, drying and disinfectants for years?","choices":["The plasmid","The capsule","The flagellum","The endospore"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is a dormant, dehydrated form with a tough protective coat that withstands conditions which kill the vegetative cell. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 30-36"},{"q":"Which structure allows a bacterium to resist being engulfed by a phagocyte?","choices":["The capsule","The endospore","Fimbriae","The ribosome"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Its slippery polysaccharide layer prevents the phagocyte from gripping the surface, so the cell cannot be taken up. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 30-36"},{"q":"What does calling a medium 'selective' mean?","choices":["It suppresses the growth of unwanted organisms","It shows visible differences between organisms","It supplies extra nutrients for fastidious organisms","It maintains a constant incubation temperature"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Something in the medium, such as a high salt concentration, prevents organisms other than the ones sought from growing at all. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 43-44"},{"q":"Which three results are read from blood agar?","choices":["Growth, no growth and partial growth","Acid, gas and no change","Coagulase, catalase and oxidase","Gamma, alpha and beta haemolysis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Each organism digests the haemoglobin to a different degree — none, partial, or complete — and the plate shows that difference directly. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 43-44"},{"q":"Bacterial growth refers to an increase in what?","choices":["Cell number","Cell size","Colony diameter","Genome size"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The individual cells stay much the same size; what rises is how many of them there are, through repeated binary fission. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 37-40"},{"q":"Why does a bacterial population rise exponentially rather than arithmetically?","choices":["Each division doubles the population","Each cell grows at a constant rate","Nutrients are consumed at a constant rate","Cells divide faster as the population grows"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Binary fission turns one cell into two, so the count goes 2, 4, 8, 16 rather than climbing by a fixed amount each generation. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 37-40"},{"q":"In which phase of the growth curve are bacteria most vulnerable to control methods?","choices":["Death phase","Lag phase","Stationary phase","Log phase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Most antimicrobials act on processes only running in a dividing cell, such as wall synthesis, so the fastest-dividing population is the most exposed. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 37-40"},{"q":"What does a bacterium gain when a phage genome integrates into its chromosome?","choices":["Loss of its plasmids","Immediate lysis of neighbouring cells","New traits, such as a toxin gene","A thicker cell wall"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The prophage is copied along with the chromosome, so a harmless-looking organism can acquire and pass on a toxin gene, which is how diphtheria and cholera toxins arise. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 52-56"},{"q":"What must happen after an animal virus enters a host cell that does not happen with a phage?","choices":["The genome must be injected","The virion must be uncoated","The cell must be lysed","The genome must be reversed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The whole particle is taken in, so the capsid has to be stripped away before the genetic material can be used; a phage injects its genome and leaves the coat outside. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A disinfectant reduces a population from 10,000,000 organisms to 10,000. How many decimal reductions is that?","choices":["One thousand","Four","Six","Three"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Each decimal reduction removes ninety per cent of whatever remains, so a thousandfold fall is three tenfold steps. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slides 62-68"},{"q":"A drug has a toxic dose for half the population of 400 milligrams and an effective dose for half the population of 50 milligrams. What is its therapeutic index?","choices":["350","0.125","8","450"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The index is the toxic dose divided by the effective dose, so 400 over 50 gives 8. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 18-20"},{"q":"What does a LARGER therapeutic index tell you about a drug?","choices":["It is safer","It is more potent","It acts more quickly","It is more likely to be bactericidal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A wide gap between the dose that works and the dose that harms leaves more room for error in dosing and for variation between patients. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 18-20"},{"q":"An antibiotic course is followed by an infection that no longer responds to it. What has happened to the bacterial population?","choices":["The immune system spared the resistant cells","The antibiotic caused resistance genes to form","The bacteria learned to avoid the drug","Resistant organisms already present were selected for"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The drug removes the susceptible competition rather than creating anything, so the few organisms that already carried resistance are left to multiply unopposed. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 45-52"},{"q":"Which resistance mechanism destroys the antibiotic molecule itself?","choices":["Target modification","An efflux pump","Enzymatic inactivation","Reduced permeability"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An enzyme such as beta-lactamase chemically cleaves the drug, so it can no longer bind its target however much of it is present. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 45-52"},{"q":"Which resistance mechanism keeps the antibiotic from ever reaching a useful concentration inside the cell?","choices":["Target modification","Enzymatic inactivation","Efflux pumping","Biofilm formation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pump exports the drug as fast as it enters, so it never accumulates to a level that would inhibit its target. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 45-52"},{"q":"Which bacterial target is completely absent from human cells?","choices":["The peptidoglycan cell wall","The ribosome","The plasma membrane","Deoxyribonucleic acid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nothing in a human cell corresponds to it, which is why drugs that attack it are among the safest antimicrobials available. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 24-32"},{"q":"Which step do the penicillins block?","choices":["Protein synthesis at the ribosome","Crosslinking of the cell wall","Folate synthesis","Deoxyribonucleic acid replication"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The beta-lactam ring inactivates the transpeptidase that links peptidoglycan strands, so the wall cannot be completed and the cell bursts. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 24-32"},{"q":"Which drug class acts at the bacterial ribosome?","choices":["The polymyxins","The penicillins","The aminoglycosides","The sulfonamides"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They bind the bacterial ribosome and halt translation, which is selective because the bacterial ribosome differs in size from the human one. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 24-32"},{"q":"Why are there fewer drugs available against fungal and protozoal infections than against bacterial ones?","choices":["Those pathogens are eukaryotes, like us","Those pathogens reproduce too slowly","Those infections resolve on their own","Those pathogens have tougher cell walls"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Selective toxicity needs a difference to exploit, and a eukaryotic pathogen's structure and physiology closely resemble our own, so safe targets are scarce. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 12-16"},{"q":"What happens if doses of an antibiotic are spaced too far apart?","choices":["The drug accumulates and becomes toxic","Organisms regrow between doses","Resistance genes are created by the low level","Absorption from the gut is reduced"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Clearance removes the drug continuously, so once the concentration drops below what inhibits the organism the suppressed population resumes multiplying. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 21-23"},{"q":"What does a bacteriostatic drug do?","choices":["Kills the organism outright","Inhibits growth without killing","Blocks the organism from attaching to host cells","Prevents the organism from acquiring resistance genes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It halts replication and leaves the host's immune system to clear the organisms that are already there. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 33-36"},{"q":"In which patient does choosing a bactericidal rather than a bacteriostatic drug matter most?","choices":["The neutropenic patient","The elderly patient","The patient with a penicillin allergy","The patient with kidney impairment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A bacteriostatic agent relies on host immunity to finish the job, and a patient without functioning neutrophils cannot supply it. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 33-36"},{"q":"A patient develops profuse diarrhoea after a broad-spectrum antibiotic course. What has occurred?","choices":["Hypersensitivity","Direct toxicity","Superinfection","Resistance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Removing the resident flora frees the niche they had been occupying, letting a resistant organism overgrow where it previously could not. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 55-58"},{"q":"Who first observed the inhibition of bacterial growth by a contaminating mould?","choices":["Fleming","Florey","Chain","Ehrlich"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. He noticed the zone of inhibition around the mould on a culture plate, which is the observation that opened the way to penicillin. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slides 5-10"},{"q":"By what mechanism does resident flora protect the host?","choices":["It raises local temperature to inhibit invaders","It secretes antibody against pathogens","It digests pathogens as they arrive","It occupies the niche first"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Competition for space and nutrients means a newly arrived pathogen finds nowhere to establish and nothing left to consume. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 8-16"},{"q":"Which body site carries the largest resident microbial population?","choices":["Small intestine","Oral cavity","Nasal passages","Large intestine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The colon holds by far the densest population in the body, which is why disturbing it with antibiotics has such marked consequences. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 8-16"},{"q":"Which of these is normally sterile?","choices":["Conjunctiva","Large intestine","Distal urethra","Cerebrospinal fluid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is an internal compartment that no organism should reach, which is why any growth from it is significant. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 8-16"},{"q":"What defines a TRUE pathogen?","choices":["It is always Gram-negative","It sickens a healthy person","It always produces an exotoxin","It is acquired only in hospital"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It carries enough virulence to overcome intact defences, which is precisely what an opportunist cannot do. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 18-24"},{"q":"Under which circumstance does an opportunist cause disease?","choices":["When it is exposed to an antibiotic","When it acquires a capsule","When the host has already had the infection","When host defences are compromised"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An organism that is harmless as resident flora becomes pathogenic once the defences holding it in check fail, or once it reaches a site it does not belong in. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 18-24"},{"q":"When is endotoxin released?","choices":["When the organism is phagocytosed intact","Continuously, secreted by the living cell","Only during sporulation","When the bacterial cell lyses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is a structural component of the Gram-negative outer membrane, so it only reaches the host once the cell breaks apart. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 40-46"},{"q":"Which organisms produce endotoxin?","choices":["All bacteria equally","Gram-positive bacteria","Fungi","Gram-negative bacteria"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Endotoxin is the lipid A portion of lipopolysaccharide, which is built into the Gram-negative outer membrane and found nowhere else. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 40-46"},{"q":"Which structure allows a bacterium to attach to host tissue?","choices":["The capsule","Fimbriae","The endospore","The ribosome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. They are the adhesins that anchor the organism to a specific receptor on the host cell, without which it would simply be washed away. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 30-38"},{"q":"What distinguishes a sign from a symptom?","choices":["A sign is treatable; a symptom is not","A sign is felt by the patient; a symptom is observed","A sign appears early; a symptom appears late","A sign is observed; a symptom is felt by the patient"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The distinction is who perceives it, which is why a sign can be recorded objectively and a symptom must be reported. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 50-58"},{"q":"What is a sequela?","choices":["A second infection following the first","Lasting damage after the illness","The interval before symptoms appear","A relapse of the original illness"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is the permanent tissue or organ injury left behind once the acute illness has resolved, such as blindness following gonococcal conjunctivitis. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slides 50-58"},{"q":"How many links make up the chain of infection?","choices":["Eight","Four","Six","Three"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Agent, reservoir, portal of exit, mode of transmission, portal of entry and susceptible host, and breaking any one of them interrupts spread. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slides 6-14"},{"q":"Which link in the chain of infection does hand hygiene break?","choices":["The susceptible host","The reservoir","The mode of transmission","The portal of exit"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Washing removes organisms from the hands that would otherwise carry them from one host to the next, which is the link most readily interrupted. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slides 6-14"},{"q":"What is a fomite?","choices":["A contaminated non-living object","A living carrier showing no symptoms","An airborne droplet nucleus","An insect that transmits disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is any inanimate item that carries organisms from one person to another, such as a stethoscope or a keyboard. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slides 40-52"},{"q":"What distinguishes a PASSIVE carrier from an active one?","choices":["The passive carrier has been treated","The passive carrier shows no symptoms","The passive carrier is not infected","The passive carrier transmits only by air"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They simply transfer organisms on hands or clothing without the organism ever establishing in them, which is why hand hygiene matters so much among staff. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slides 16-26"},{"q":"What separates a vector from a reservoir?","choices":["The vector carries the organism; the reservoir harbours it","The vector harbours the organism; the reservoir carries it","The vector is inanimate; the reservoir is living","The vector is always an insect; the reservoir always an animal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The reservoir is where the organism normally lives and multiplies, while the vector merely transports it to a new host. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slides 16-26"},{"q":"Why can a zoonotic infection not be eradicated the way smallpox was?","choices":["Zoonotic organisms resist all antimicrobials","Zoonotic organisms mutate too quickly","Zoonotic infections are always viral","The animal reservoir persists"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Eradication requires removing every place the organism can live, and an infection maintained in animals continues however many human cases are cured. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slides 28-36"},{"q":"Which patients are most at risk from hospital-acquired infection?","choices":["Outpatients attending clinics","The young and otherwise healthy","The immunocompromised","Patients on short admissions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Impaired defences allow organisms of low virulence, including the patient's own flora, to establish infection they could not otherwise cause. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slides 40-46"},{"q":"What determines whether a disinfectant actually works on a surface?","choices":["Whether the surface looks clean","Its concentration and contact time","The temperature of the room","The brand of the product"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An agent applied too dilute, or wiped away before its contact time has elapsed, will not achieve the reduction it is rated for however good the chemical is. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slides 56-64"},{"q":"Which mechanism of horizontal gene transfer uses a virus as the carrier?","choices":["Conjugation","Transformation","Transduction","Binary fission"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A bacteriophage packages host genetic material and injects it into the next cell, carrying resistance genes between organisms that never touch. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slides 66-72"},{"q":"Which of the three lines of defense is the specific one?","choices":["All three equally","The first","The second","The third"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Only the third is acquired through exposure to a particular antigen and carries memory of it; the first two act identically against anything. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Into which first-line category does lysozyme fall?","choices":["Microbiological","Physical","Chemical","Genetic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is an enzyme that digests peptidoglycan, attacking the invader chemically rather than obstructing it. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 13-18"},{"q":"Into which first-line category does the resident flora fall?","choices":["Chemical","Microbiological","Physical","Genetic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Protection comes from living organisms occupying the niche and competing for nutrients, which is neither a physical nor a chemical mechanism. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 13-18"},{"q":"Lysozyme strips the entire wall from which organisms?","choices":["Fungi","Gram-negative organisms","Gram-positive organisms","Both Gram-positive and Gram-negative equally"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The intuition that a thicker wall is sturdier is backwards here: their peptidoglycan is the enzyme's substrate and lies exposed as the outermost layer. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 13-18"},{"q":"What does a pathogen-associated molecular pattern represent?","choices":["A conserved microbial structure","A molecule released by injured host cells","A specific antigen unique to one organism","An antibody bound to a microbial surface"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Because these structures are shared across whole classes of organism and absent from us, a small set of receptors can detect almost any invader. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 20-30"},{"q":"What does a damage-associated molecular pattern signal?","choices":["Successful clearance of an infection","The presence of a Gram-negative organism","Injury to the host's own cells","Antibody production by plasma cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Molecules normally kept inside a cell appear outside when it is damaged, so the same receptor system provokes inflammation after sterile injury as after infection. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 20-30"},{"q":"Which leukocyte is the most numerous?","choices":["The monocyte","The lymphocyte","The neutrophil","The eosinophil"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Neutrophils make up roughly 55 to 90 per cent of the differential, which is why a raised total count so often reflects a neutrophil response. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 66-70"},{"q":"What is the eosinophil's principal role?","choices":["Antibody production","Release of histamine","Antigen presentation to lymphocytes","Defence against parasites"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Its granules carry proteins toxic to organisms far too large to phagocytose, particularly helminths, which are released into the surrounding space. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 66-70"},{"q":"What is the basophil's principal role?","choices":["Defence against helminths","Release of chemical mediators","Phagocytosis of bacteria","Killing virus-infected cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It degranulates to release histamine and related mediators, driving the vascular changes of inflammation and the allergic response. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 66-70"},{"q":"What can a macrophage do that a neutrophil cannot?","choices":["Release histamine","Phagocytose bacteria","Migrate towards a chemical signal","Present antigen to lymphocytes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. This is the step that recruits the specific immune system, which is why the macrophage bridges innate and adaptive defence while the neutrophil simply kills and dies. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 66-72"},{"q":"What does the differential add that a total white cell count alone cannot give?","choices":["Whether the cell types are in normal proportion","The total number of white cells, more precisely","The identity of the infecting organism","The antibody titre against the organism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A normal total can conceal a badly skewed mix, and it is the proportion that distinguishes a bacterial from a viral pattern. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 70-74"},{"q":"Which set lists the four classical signs of inflammation?","choices":["Heat, swelling, bleeding and numbness","Redness, heat, pus and fever","Swelling, pain, itching and scaling","Redness, heat, swelling and pain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Rubor, calor, tumor and dolor, with loss of function sometimes added as a fifth. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 34-40"},{"q":"What produces the redness and heat of inflammation?","choices":["Vasoconstriction concentrating blood in the area","Vasodilation increasing blood flow to the area","Neutrophils releasing pigment into the tissue","Fluid leaking into the interstitial space"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Widening the supplying arterioles brings more warm blood into the injured region, which accounts for both the colour and the temperature. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 34-40"},{"q":"What causes fever?","choices":["Endotoxin acting directly on the skin","Dehydration raising core temperature","Histamine released from mast cells","Cytokines reset the set point"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Interleukin-1, interleukin-6 and tumour necrosis factor act systemically on the hypothalamus, which then defends a higher target temperature. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 40-46"},{"q":"Why is fever beneficial?","choices":["It kills all pathogens outright","It inhibits pathogen growth","It prevents the pathogen from entering cells","It stimulates antibody production exclusively"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Many organisms replicate poorly above normal body temperature, while the host's own enzymatic and cellular responses run faster. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 40-46"},{"q":"Why is a high fever dangerous?","choices":["High temperatures damage tissue","It suppresses the immune response","It causes the pathogen to mutate","It prevents antibiotics from working"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Above a certain point host proteins begin to denature, so the same response that inhibits the organism starts to harm the patient. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 40-46"},{"q":"What does interferon do to a virus?","choices":["It destroys viral particles directly","It prevents spread rather than killing the virus","It coats the virus so phagocytes engulf it","It lyses the infected cell immediately"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It acts on neighbouring uninfected cells, inducing an antiviral state so the infection cannot propagate; the already-infected cell is not rescued. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 48-52"},{"q":"In what order are the three complement pathways activated?","choices":["Classical, then lectin, then alternative","Alternative, then lectin, then classical","Lectin, then alternative, then classical","Classical, then alternative, then lectin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The alternative pathway fires immediately with no prior recognition needed, the lectin pathway follows, and the classical comes last because it waits on antibody. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 42-46"},{"q":"Which component do all three complement pathways converge on?","choices":["C1","C3","C9","Mannose-binding lectin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pathways differ only in how they are switched on; all three end by cleaving C3, which is why it is the pivotal molecule of the cascade. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 42-46"},{"q":"Why does pus form at a site of bacterial infection?","choices":["Bacteria secrete it as a waste product","Macrophages migrate in and release pigment","Spent neutrophils die at the site","Plasma leaks from dilated capillaries"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Each neutrophil carries one load of granule contents and no means of making more, so the cells are spent by their own killing and accumulate as purulent material. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slides 76-78"},{"q":"A newborn receives antibody across the placenta. Which type of immunity is that?","choices":["Artificial passive","Natural active","Natural passive","Artificial active"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nothing was engineered, so it is natural, and the infant receives ready-made antibody rather than producing it, so it is passive and temporary. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 6-12"},{"q":"Which type of immunity does vaccination produce?","choices":["Artificial active","Artificial passive","Natural active","Natural passive"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The antigen is administered deliberately, so it is artificial, and the recipient mounts their own response and forms memory, so it is active. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 6-12"},{"q":"How many chains make up an antibody monomer?","choices":["Four","Two","Six","Eight"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Two identical heavy chains and two identical light chains form the Y-shaped molecule. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 40-48"},{"q":"Which antibody class appears first in a new infection?","choices":["Immunoglobulin M","Immunoglobulin G","Immunoglobulin A","Immunoglobulin E"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is produced first and is by far the largest, its ten binding sites making it highly effective at agglutinating antigen early on. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 50-60"},{"q":"Which antibody class guards mucosal surfaces?","choices":["Immunoglobulin M","Immunoglobulin A","Immunoglobulin D","Immunoglobulin E"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is secreted across epithelium onto the mucous membranes of the gut, airway and urogenital tract, where it blocks attachment. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 50-60"},{"q":"When are lymphocyte receptor specificities generated?","choices":["After the antigen arrives, in response to it","Before any antigen has entered","Only during an active infection","Continuously, in proportion to antigen exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The repertoire is produced during lymphocyte development by gene rearrangement, so an arriving antigen selects a clone that already exists rather than instructing anything. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 30-38"},{"q":"Class two major histocompatibility complex molecules present antigen to which cell?","choices":["The helper T cell","The cytotoxic T cell","The B cell","The natural killer cell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Class two is carried by antigen-presenting cells and displays engulfed material to the helper subset, which bears the matching cluster of differentiation 4 marker. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 20-28"},{"q":"What does opsonisation do?","choices":["Coats the organism so phagocytes can engulf it","Blocks the organism's attachment sites","Clumps organisms together","Punches holes in the organism's membrane"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The coating marks the target and gives the phagocyte a surface it can bind, which greatly increases the rate of engulfment. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 62-70"},{"q":"How does a second exposure to the same antigen differ from the first?","choices":["It is identical to the first","It is slower and produces more immunoglobulin M","It is faster and produces far more immunoglobulin G","It is weaker, because tolerance has developed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Memory cells laid down by the first exposure are already present and pre-committed, so the response begins sooner, climbs higher and switches class. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slides 72-80"}]};
